DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       eevveennttffdd()  creates  an  "eventfd  object"  that can be used as an event
       wait/notify mechanism by user‐space applications, and by the kernel  to
       notify  user‐space  applications of events.  The object contains an un‐
       signed 64‐bit integer (_u_i_n_t_6_4___t) counter that is maintained by the ker‐
       nel.  This counter is initialized with the value specified in the argu‐
       ment _i_n_i_t_v_a_l.

       As its return value, eevveennttffdd() returns a new file descriptor  that  can
       be used to refer to the eventfd object.

       The  following values may be bitwise ORed in _f_l_a_g_s to change the behav‐
       ior of eevveennttffdd():

       EEFFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC (since Linux 2.6.27)
              Set the close‐on‐exec (FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC) flag on the new file descrip‐
              tor.  See the description of the OO__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag in  ooppeenn(2)  for
              reasons why this may be useful.

       EEFFDD__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK (since Linux 2.6.27)
              Set the OO__NNOONNBBLLOOCCKK file status flag on the open file description
              (see  ooppeenn(2))  referred  to  by the new file descriptor.  Using
              this flag saves extra calls to ffccnnttll(2) to achieve the same  re‐
              sult.

       EEFFDD__SSEEMMAAPPHHOORREE (since Linux 2.6.30)
              Provide semaphore‐like semantics for reads from the new file de‐
              scriptor.  See below.

       Up to Linux 2.6.26, the _f_l_a_g_s argument is unused, and must be specified
       as zero.

       The  following  operations  can be performed on the file descriptor re‐
       turned by eevveennttffdd():

       rreeaadd(2)
              Each successful rreeaadd(2) returns an 8‐byte  integer.   A  rreeaadd(2)
              fails  with  the error EEIINNVVAALL if the size of the supplied buffer
              is less than 8 bytes.

              The value returned by rreeaadd(2) is in host byte order—that is, the
              native byte order for integers on the host machine.

              The semantics of rreeaadd(2) depend on whether the  eventfd  counter
              currently has a nonzero value and whether the EEFFDD__SSEEMMAAPPHHOORREE flag
              was specified when creating the eventfd file descriptor:

              •  If  EEFFDD__SSEEMMAAPPHHOORREE  was  not specified and the eventfd counter
                 has a nonzero value, then a rreeaadd(2) returns 8 bytes  contain‐
                 ing that value, and the counter’s value is reset to zero.

              •  If  EEFFDD__SSEEMMAAPPHHOORREE was specified and the eventfd counter has a
                 nonzero value, then a rreeaadd(2) returns 8 bytes containing  the
                 value 1, and the counter’s value is decremented by 1.

              •  If  the  eventfd  counter  is zero at the time of the call to
                 rreeaadd(2), then the call either blocks until  the  counter  be‐
                 comes  nonzero  (at  which  time, the rreeaadd(2) proceeds as de‐
                 scribed above) or fails with the error EEAAGGAAIINN if the file de‐
                 scriptor has been made nonblocking.

       wwrriittee(2)
              A wwrriittee(2) call adds the 8‐byte integer value  supplied  in  its
              buffer  to the counter.  The maximum value that may be stored in
              the counter is the largest unsigned 64‐bit value minus 1  (i.e.,
              0xfffffffffffffffe).   If the addition would cause the counter’s
              value to exceed the maximum, then the wwrriittee(2) either blocks un‐
              til a rreeaadd(2) is performed on the file descriptor, or fails with
              the error EEAAGGAAIINN if the file descriptor has been made  nonblock‐
              ing.

              A  wwrriittee(2)  fails with the error EEIINNVVAALL if the size of the sup‐
              plied buffer is less than 8 bytes, or if an attempt is  made  to
              write the value 0xffffffffffffffff.

       ppoollll(2)
       sseelleecctt(2)
       (and similar)
              The  returned  file descriptor supports ppoollll(2) (and analogously
              eeppoollll(7)) and sseelleecctt(2), as follows:

              •  The file descriptor is readable (the sseelleecctt(2) _r_e_a_d_f_d_s  argu‐
                 ment;  the  ppoollll(2)  PPOOLLLLIINN  flag) if the counter has a value
                 greater than 0.

              •  The file descriptor is writable (the sseelleecctt(2) _w_r_i_t_e_f_d_s argu‐
                 ment; the ppoollll(2) PPOOLLLLOOUUTT flag) if it is possible to write  a
                 value of at least "1" without blocking.

              •  If  an  overflow  of the counter value was detected, then ssee‐‐
                 lleecctt(2) indicates the file descriptor as being both  readable
                 and  writable, and ppoollll(2) returns a PPOOLLLLEERRRR event.  As noted
                 above, wwrriittee(2) can never overflow the counter.   However  an
                 overflow  can  occur if 2^64 eventfd "signal posts" were per‐
                 formed by the KAIO  subsystem  (theoretically  possible,  but
                 practically  unlikely).   If  an  overflow has occurred, then
                 rreeaadd(2)  will  return  that  maximum  _u_i_n_t_6_4___t  value  (i.e.,
                 0xffffffffffffffff).

              The  eventfd  file  descriptor  also supports the other file‐de‐
              scriptor multiplexing APIs: ppsseelleecctt(2) and ppppoollll(2).

       cclloossee(2)
              When the file descriptor is no  longer  required  it  should  be
              closed.   When  all  file  descriptors  associated with the same
              eventfd object have been closed, the resources  for  object  are
              freed by the kernel.

       A  copy of the file descriptor created by eevveennttffdd() is inherited by the
       child produced by ffoorrkk(2).  The duplicate file descriptor is associated
       with the same eventfd object.  File descriptors  created  by  eevveennttffdd()
       are  preserved across eexxeeccvvee(2), unless the close‐on‐exec flag has been
       set.

